@startuml architecture
' Component view: the HAL / app layering. The application logic never touches
' a register, so it is portable and host-testable; only the HAL does I/O.
'
' Grounded in the real source: Src/main.c, Src/telemetry.c, Src/board.c,
' Src/uart.c, Src/adc.c, Inc/registers.h.

title hmi-edge-node: HAL / app layering

skinparam shadowing false
skinparam componentStyle rectangle

package "Composition" {
  [main.c] as Main
}

package "Application (pure logic, host-testable)" {
  [telemetry]   as Tel
  [temperature] as Temp
}

package "HAL / BSP (the only code that touches registers)" {
  [board] as Board
  [uart]  as Uart
  [adc]   as Adc
  [registers.h] as Regs
}

package "Hardware (STM32L476)" {
  [GPIO: button B1 / LED LD2] as Gpio
  [USART2 -> ST-Link VCP]     as Usart
  [ADC1: internal temp sensor] as AdcHw
}

Main --> Board : init / button / led
Main --> Uart  : init / send
Main --> Adc   : init / read counts + factory cal
Main --> Temp  : temperature_deci_celsius(cal,\nts_raw, vrefint_raw)
Main --> Tel   : telemetry_update(button,\nread_temp, sink)

Tel ..> Main : read_temp() injected\n(the adapter that joins adc + temperature)
Tel ..> Uart : sink() injected

Board --> Regs
Uart  --> Regs
Adc   --> Regs

Board --> Gpio
Uart  --> Usart
Adc   --> AdcHw

note bottom of Tel
  Depends only on injected function
  pointers, never on registers.
  Unit-testable on a host PC.
end note

note bottom of Temp
  Pure integer arithmetic, no FPU.
  The driver reports counts, this
  decides what they mean, main.c
  joins the two. That split is what
  makes the maths host-testable.
end note

@enduml
